WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.620
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers episode 92 coming to you on June 5th, 2026.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.940
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.620
An interstellar comet is venting methane at levels never seen in solar system comets,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.400
hinting at a wildly different formation history.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.920
The James Webb Space Telescope detected large amounts of methane around three eye atlas.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.440
This finding shows the object behaves differently from any comet observed in our solar system.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.720
The methane release points to distinct surface or interior chemistry shaped outside our own planetary

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.440
environment. Follow-up spectra from Webb are expected to clarify whether similar molecules

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.520
accompany the methane. While this distant object reveals alien chemistry, closer to home,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.180
we can watch two familiar planets put on a show in the evening sky.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:56.320
A new JWST technique separates sand clouds on the morning side from clear evening skies on the

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:03.840
gas giant WASP 94AB. Transmission spectra taken at different orbital phases made the distinction

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:11.720
possible. Morning hemispheres show sand clouds while evening skies appear clear. Revised atmospheric

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.400
retrievals indicate lower oxygen and carbon abundances than earlier estimates.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.620
The approach improves composition measurements for future transmission spectra.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.260
From one planet's weather patterns, we now zoom out to the vast magnetic structures that thread the

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:35.320
entire cosmos. The new polarization survey reveals magnetic fields on previously unmapped cosmic

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:42.240
scales. These fields arise from tiny seed fields amplified by the motion of ionized gas during

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.140
structure formation. The motion stretches and folds the fields over billions of years,

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.540
until they reach strength of a few nano-gas on mega-parsec scales.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.100
The coherence length of some of these fields exceeds 10 million light years,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.880
larger than the distance to the nearest major galaxy cluster. We can measure the fields through

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:09.040
synchrotronic mission and ferridae rotation, yet no one has explained why the amplification saturates

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:15.660
at the observed level instead of growing stronger or decaying away. While these immense fields shape

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.700
galaxies, our own solar system offers simpler but still striking sights this week.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.600
Bright Venus and Jupiter pass through conjunction this week. Mercury,

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:31.840
Pollocks, and Castor remained visible nearby in the same twilight sky. Observers contract the

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.020
changing separation each evening through June 14. The geometry offers a clear view of the two brightest

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:44.060
planets from northern latitudes. After enjoying the twilight planets, early risers can catch another

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:50.400
world climbing higher before dawn. The telescope recorded young stars spanning the full sequence from

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:57.840
collapsing cores to emerging clusters. Multiple wavelengths isolate dusk acoons, jets, and disks around

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.200
the proto-stars. The data set supplies a single region timeline of early stellar evolution.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:09.460
From star birth, we turn to the red planet's steady progress in our morning sky.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.800
The red planet now clears the horizon roughly an hour before sunrise. Its position allows

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:22.460
extended observation time above atmospheric distortion. Weekly tracking shows steady progress toward

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:28.260
opposition later in the year. While Mars rewards early risers, space weather may bring another

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:34.000
treat for northern observers. Multiple coronal mass ejections are directed toward Earth.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.700
The combined arrival could produce visible aurora across mid-latitudes states Thursday night.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.280
Observers are advised to watch the northern horizon after local midnight.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.880
From fleeting sky displays, we moved a long-term records of Jupiter's atmosphere.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.260
The spacecraft captured the image during its 61st close flyby on May 12, 2024.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.240
Enhanced colors highlight chaotic cloud bands and oval features in the northern hemisphere.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:09.560
The data add to Juno's long-term record of atmospheric dynamics. Juno's atmospheric data also

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.480
offer clues about cosmic rays, which will explore next. Measurements from the mission's instruments

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.640
link particle events at Jupiter to broader galactic processes. The findings refine models of how

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:28.520
cosmic rays interact with planetary magnetospheres. Additional passes will test whether the pattern holds

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:34.960
at different latitudes. While Juno gathers science, hardware for future human missions is also moving.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.960
Eight solid rocket motor segments are en route from Northrop Grumman's facility in Corin, Utah.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.280
The hardware will complete stacking for the space launch system vehicle at Kennedy Space Center.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.660
The shipment marks the end of booster production for the mission.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.480
American rockets are not the only ones active, a Chinese launch recently added more satellites.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:04.040
The rocket launched at 0634 UTC on June 5 carrying the group 12 batch of internet satellites.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.200
The mission adds to China's growing low-earth orbit constellation.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.120
Deployment occurred from the coastal launch site. Commercial stations themselves are also

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:20.520
expanding partnerships beyond US borders. American companies are extending commercial low-earth

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:27.140
orbit platforms to include European partners. The move broadens access beyond current US focused

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:34.220
facilities. Agreements target shared utilization after the ISS era. While new stations take shape,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.280
earth observation satellites continue delivering practical images. The image captures the capital

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:47.040
of Azerbaijan, host of this year's world environment day celebrations. Sentinel-2's multi-spectral

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.600
data reveal urban layout and surrounding coastal features. The view highlights how satellite

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:58.360
monitoring supports environmental awareness events. Similar satellite views are also tracking

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:04.600
controlled burns in Australia. Sentinel and Landsat imagery track controlled fires set in the

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.220
northern territory. The burns reduce fuel loads ahead of peak fire season.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:15.340
Satellite monitoring quantifies burned area and smoke transport in near real time.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:20.880
Finally, new research examines why some galaxies stopped forming stars early.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:26.820
Researchers traced rapid quenching in the most massive systems to intense early feedback.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:33.640
The findings come from combined simulation and observational data sets. The work narrows

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:39.920
the window when star formation shuts down in these objects. New data keep arriving from both near

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:46.340
earth orbiters and distant telescopes. Keep an eye on follow-up spectra from web on the interstellar

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:52.700
comet as new observations come in next week. That covers today's space and science news. Share

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:58.040
this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick and Vancouver. See you

